      "ospf_teaching": {
        "prerequisite_note": "Before editing, understand IPv4 prefixes, local interface addresses and outgoing route costs. This native model derives current compatibility and routes; it does not run OSPF packets or report an observed Full adjacency.",
        "concepts": [
          {
            "title": "Current P2P compatibility",
            "text": "Both enabled, up P2P endpoints need usable numbering, distinct explicit router IDs, the same area, and matching Hello and Dead parameters. Parameters are compared without sending packets or waiting for protocol timers. P2P mask differences are warnings rather than Hello blockers."
          },
          {
            "title": "Local process IDs and opaque router IDs",
            "text": "Process IDs are local labels and may differ between routers. A router ID is an opaque unique32-bit number, written with dots; it need not be assigned to an interface or be an ordinary host address. This model distinguishes an omitted ID from explicit zero and treats both as non-running."
          },
          {
            "title": "Advertised passive networks and costs",
            "text": "A passive interface can advertise its attached prefix while forming no peer on that interface. A remote SPF candidate adds outgoing interface costs along the path and the advertising destination interface cost. Multiple equal-cost first-hop choices remain a set; they are not one invented packet path."
          }
        ],
        "example_description": "PracticeLeft uses Hello5 while PracticeRight uses Hello10. Keep the existing addresses, up flags and costs, match the P2P Hello parameter, and verify a current remote candidate for172.20.1.0/24. The default cost is4+6=10: PracticeLeft uplink plus PracticeRight passive LAN. These fixture names and values are separate from the assignment.",
